WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:02.100
Instructor: Okay, so in the previous video,

00:02.100 --> 00:06.210
we talked about blobs, and it's kind of probably confusing

00:06.210 --> 00:07.590
at this point.

00:07.590 --> 00:11.460
We said blobs store the contents of files,

00:11.460 --> 00:12.990
but not even the file name.

00:12.990 --> 00:15.000
Just the contents of files.

00:15.000 --> 00:16.530
Now, that's fine, I guess,

00:16.530 --> 00:19.380
if we wanna do things like we have been doing,

00:19.380 --> 00:22.260
where we're storing a couple of words,

00:22.260 --> 00:24.360
or the content of a single file,

00:24.360 --> 00:25.530
and then spitting it back out,

00:25.530 --> 00:27.900
asking Git to return it back to us.

00:27.900 --> 00:30.840
But in a large project, with hundreds of files

00:30.840 --> 00:34.050
in nested folders, and the crazy structure,

00:34.050 --> 00:36.840
if I can just tell Git, check out this commit,

00:36.840 --> 00:39.000
or switch to this branch, and it can change

00:39.000 --> 00:41.310
all of those files, all of those folders,

00:41.310 --> 00:43.260
it might delete some in my working directory.

00:43.260 --> 00:45.450
It might add a whole bunch of stuff.

00:45.450 --> 00:47.970
How is it able to keep track of that structure?

00:47.970 --> 00:49.860
It's not just the insides of the files,

00:49.860 --> 00:52.770
it's the relationships between the files.

00:52.770 --> 00:55.440
It's the structure of the directories.

00:55.440 --> 00:57.900
So that's the role of tree objects.

00:57.900 --> 01:00.600
Trees are Git objects that are used to store

01:00.600 --> 01:02.640
the contents of a directory.

01:02.640 --> 01:05.520
So blobs store the contents of a file,

01:05.520 --> 01:08.520
and trees store the contents of a directory.

01:08.520 --> 01:11.400
So trees actually contain pointers

01:11.400 --> 01:15.390
that can refer both to blobs and to other trees.

01:15.390 --> 01:16.800
So think about it this way.

01:16.800 --> 01:19.350
If we have one folder,

01:19.350 --> 01:22.980
and inside that one folder, there is one file,

01:22.980 --> 01:25.920
well, Git would represent that with one tree,

01:25.920 --> 01:29.940
and that tree would point to, or refer to, one blob

01:29.940 --> 01:31.410
for that file.

01:31.410 --> 01:35.430
So each entry in a tree, you can visualize it like this,

01:35.430 --> 01:36.600
contains the type.

01:36.600 --> 01:38.670
Is it a blob or a tree?

01:38.670 --> 01:41.460
The two things that a tree can refer to,

01:41.460 --> 01:43.080
or can reference.

01:43.080 --> 01:46.140
And then for each entry, we've got the hash.

01:46.140 --> 01:47.820
So this is not the commit hash.

01:47.820 --> 01:50.700
This is a blob hash, in this case.

01:50.700 --> 01:51.741
Right?

01:51.741 --> 01:52.800
We saw a blob, like this one.

01:52.800 --> 01:53.820
1F7.

01:53.820 --> 01:56.100
It's the contents of some file.

01:56.100 --> 01:57.750
Well, here's a tree.

01:57.750 --> 02:01.950
And it references that contents, that blob, 1F7,

02:01.950 --> 02:03.840
and it gives it a name.

02:03.840 --> 02:05.760
App.js.

02:05.760 --> 02:08.790
And then down here, it references another blob.

02:08.790 --> 02:09.990
Here's the hash.

02:09.990 --> 02:12.900
So it's not storing the contents in the tree.

02:12.900 --> 02:14.400
That's what the blob is for.

02:14.400 --> 02:15.990
This is the structure.

02:15.990 --> 02:17.760
This is the content.

02:17.760 --> 02:20.700
So we end up with references to blobs,

02:20.700 --> 02:23.340
but also references to trees,

02:23.340 --> 02:26.220
because we have nested directories, often.

02:26.220 --> 02:28.800
So in this case, in this tree,

02:28.800 --> 02:31.740
we're representing app.js, and README.

02:31.740 --> 02:33.870
Two files, two blobs.

02:33.870 --> 02:35.490
But then, there's an images directory

02:35.490 --> 02:38.790
and a styles directory, and each one of those

02:38.790 --> 02:41.250
would be represented with its own tree.

02:41.250 --> 02:44.310
And there will be blobs, most likely, in there,

02:44.310 --> 02:46.590
and then maybe more trees.

02:46.590 --> 02:48.060
So hopefully this diagram helps

02:48.060 --> 02:49.320
explain it a little bit.

02:49.320 --> 02:52.260
If this is my folder structure, two files,

02:52.260 --> 02:56.010
index.html, main.js, and then a directory,

02:56.010 --> 02:58.110
nested, called styles.

02:58.110 --> 03:02.100
Apps.css is inside, nav.css is inside.

03:02.100 --> 03:05.010
Well, Git would have a single tree to represent

03:05.010 --> 03:06.660
this entire directory.

03:06.660 --> 03:07.950
So we've got a tree.

03:07.950 --> 03:10.440
And then it has a blob that it refers to,

03:10.440 --> 03:12.870
and it names it index.html.

03:12.870 --> 03:15.180
So that's the contents of that file.

03:15.180 --> 03:18.360
Remember, the blob itself does not store the name.

03:18.360 --> 03:20.130
The tree stores the name.

03:20.130 --> 03:22.410
And then it references the hash of that blob.

03:22.410 --> 03:24.360
So this is that connection between a file name

03:24.360 --> 03:25.800
and the contents.

03:25.800 --> 03:29.010
1F7, that's this blob.

03:29.010 --> 03:30.030
Okay.

03:30.030 --> 03:32.880
So then the same thing for this main.js file.

03:32.880 --> 03:35.850
It points to that blob, whatever that hash is,

03:35.850 --> 03:37.140
and names it.

03:37.140 --> 03:41.250
But then, it refers to another tree called styles.

03:41.250 --> 03:44.280
And that tree contains two blobs.

03:44.280 --> 03:46.710
App.css refers to one blob.

03:46.710 --> 03:49.380
Nav.css refers to another blob.

03:49.380 --> 03:51.750
So here's a more fleshed out diagram,

03:51.750 --> 03:54.750
complete with the actual hashes that match.

03:54.750 --> 03:56.490
So you can see, we have a tree.

03:56.490 --> 03:58.530
This tree has its own hash.

03:58.530 --> 03:59.790
Every tree has its own hash.

03:59.790 --> 04:00.660
It's a Git object.

04:00.660 --> 04:03.780
So this is stored in the objects directory.

04:03.780 --> 04:08.780
And we see there's a blob, 1F7A7, right here.

04:08.910 --> 04:11.460
So that is hashed and stored,

04:11.460 --> 04:13.680
and is called index.html.

04:13.680 --> 04:17.400
Here's the blob, this is the tree that refers to it.

04:17.400 --> 04:20.130
Here's another blob, BE321.

04:20.130 --> 04:21.480
That matches this.

04:21.480 --> 04:22.860
And it's called main.js.

04:22.860 --> 04:24.360
It's a blob.

04:24.360 --> 04:25.950
So whatever is in that file

04:25.950 --> 04:29.070
is the contents of a file called main.js.

04:29.070 --> 04:31.350
And then we've got a tree, styles,

04:31.350 --> 04:32.760
so that's a directory.

04:32.760 --> 04:34.620
98287.

04:34.620 --> 04:35.970
That's the name of that hash.

04:35.970 --> 04:38.670
It's not the name, but that's the hash we see here.

04:38.670 --> 04:42.720
And that tree stores two blobs, 10ABB,

04:42.720 --> 04:44.310
which is app.css,

04:44.310 --> 04:49.110
and then FFE77, which is nav.css.

04:49.110 --> 04:50.880
Those are the contents there.

04:50.880 --> 04:53.580
So the last thing I'll show you in this video,

04:53.580 --> 04:55.830
we're not gonna make any trees right now,

04:55.830 --> 04:58.710
but I'm gonna show you that in an existing repo,

04:58.710 --> 05:02.010
that has history, we can view trees,

05:02.010 --> 05:06.000
just like we could use cat file to view blobs.

05:06.000 --> 05:08.040
We can use cat file to view trees,

05:08.040 --> 05:10.050
and we don't have a tree to reference

05:10.050 --> 05:12.900
in our own repository that we've been working on,

05:12.900 --> 05:15.150
but this is the react repository,

05:15.150 --> 05:16.770
or any repo we've worked on.

05:16.770 --> 05:19.470
If we have some commits that we've made,

05:19.470 --> 05:21.930
and we've added some files, and folders and stuff,

05:21.930 --> 05:24.270
we will have a tree, at least one,

05:24.270 --> 05:26.940
as we'll soon see, every commit references a tree.

05:26.940 --> 05:29.910
Anyway, this command, git cat file -P,

05:29.910 --> 05:30.900
we've already seen,

05:30.900 --> 05:35.580
but this syntax, master, and then this caret tree,

05:35.580 --> 05:38.010
that syntax is a particular way to refer

05:38.010 --> 05:41.130
to the tree object that is pointed to

05:41.130 --> 05:42.900
by the tip of our master branch.

05:42.900 --> 05:45.060
So the last commit on master,

05:45.060 --> 05:46.650
every commit refers to a tree.

05:46.650 --> 05:49.080
This just gives us the most recent tree.

05:49.080 --> 05:50.460
So let me show you that.

05:50.460 --> 05:53.490
This is that react repo that has all sorts of commits,

05:53.490 --> 05:54.600
lots of files in here.

05:54.600 --> 05:55.710
You can see them.

05:55.710 --> 05:58.320
A couple directories, okay?

05:58.320 --> 06:00.450
Really just fixtures, packages, and scripts,

06:00.450 --> 06:01.710
and then files.

06:01.710 --> 06:05.073
Now, if I do Git, cat file -P,

06:05.970 --> 06:08.100
and then I'm going to do that weird syntax.

06:08.100 --> 06:13.100
So master and then caret above the six tree.

06:14.040 --> 06:15.330
Like that.

06:15.330 --> 06:17.010
And I hit enter.

06:17.010 --> 06:20.340
It prints out, it pretty prints a lot of stuff.

06:20.340 --> 06:21.510
We could ignore this over here.

06:21.510 --> 06:22.920
It's just a file mode.

06:22.920 --> 06:23.910
Doesn't matter.

06:23.910 --> 06:26.790
But it also shows us if it's a tree or a blob,

06:26.790 --> 06:28.650
and then the hash for each,

06:28.650 --> 06:30.630
and then the name.

06:30.630 --> 06:33.000
So we can see for each file,

06:33.000 --> 06:36.030
we've got, let's see, circleCI.

06:36.030 --> 06:36.960
That's a file.

06:36.960 --> 06:38.340
Somewhere over here, it's a hidden file,

06:38.340 --> 06:39.960
so it's not showing up there.

06:39.960 --> 06:40.800
Let's keep going down.

06:40.800 --> 06:41.700
Here we are.

06:41.700 --> 06:42.630
Authors.

06:42.630 --> 06:43.830
That's a blob.

06:43.830 --> 06:44.663
Right?

06:44.663 --> 06:48.030
So the contents are stored with this hash.

06:48.030 --> 06:49.980
And I could actually view the contents

06:49.980 --> 06:51.300
if I wanted to.

06:51.300 --> 06:53.700
Let's take a look, how about, sure.

06:53.700 --> 06:54.533
The read me.

06:54.533 --> 06:57.900
I could copy this, just I'll keep it on my clipboard.

06:57.900 --> 07:00.300
But then I'll also show you there's trees, right?

07:00.300 --> 07:03.540
Here's a tree object for the fixtures directory.

07:03.540 --> 07:05.310
Here's another one for packages,

07:05.310 --> 07:07.290
and another for scripts.

07:07.290 --> 07:09.660
There we are, the three directories.

07:09.660 --> 07:11.820
So I'll just show this here.

07:11.820 --> 07:13.980
If I take this hash that I copied.

07:13.980 --> 07:15.300
This was a blob hash.

07:15.300 --> 07:20.300
Remember, I can do cat file -P, if I can type.

07:20.370 --> 07:21.270
Here we are.

07:21.270 --> 07:23.040
Pass in that hash.

07:23.040 --> 07:25.140
And it's showing me the contents of the read me.

07:25.140 --> 07:27.390
This is all the stuff from that read me file.

07:29.160 --> 07:32.190
So quite a bit of stuff that it's storing there.

07:32.190 --> 07:33.690
It's not too much.

07:33.690 --> 07:34.770
The last thing I wanna show you

07:34.770 --> 07:38.550
is that another option for this cat file command

07:38.550 --> 07:41.430
is to actually tell us the type of something.

07:41.430 --> 07:42.720
What type of Git object.

07:42.720 --> 07:45.690
If I do -T, it tells me it's a blob.

07:45.690 --> 07:47.430
But if I instead do this,

07:47.430 --> 07:50.760
Git cat-file, well, actually,

07:50.760 --> 07:52.050
why don't I just show you?

07:52.050 --> 07:54.120
If we look at the tree again,

07:54.120 --> 07:56.040
if I pick one of these trees,

07:56.040 --> 07:58.590
like this tree object that represents

07:58.590 --> 08:00.480
the fixtures directory,

08:00.480 --> 08:01.800
if I do the same thing,

08:01.800 --> 08:03.930
git cat file -T.

08:03.930 --> 08:06.540
With that, it tells me it's a tree.

08:06.540 --> 08:09.120
So there are two types we've covered so far.

08:09.120 --> 08:10.050
Blobs and trees.

08:10.050 --> 08:13.200
We haven't seen how to make a tree in the same way

08:13.200 --> 08:15.780
that we saw how to make a blob manually.

08:15.780 --> 08:17.580
I showed you an existing repository

08:17.580 --> 08:19.290
that's react repo.

08:19.290 --> 08:21.060
But all that you need to know for now

08:21.060 --> 08:24.000
is that trees represent folders.

08:24.000 --> 08:26.040
The structure of a directory,

08:26.040 --> 08:28.560
and inside of a folder, we can have files,

08:28.560 --> 08:31.020
but we can also have other folders.

08:31.020 --> 08:33.660
So the tree itself contains,

08:33.660 --> 08:35.580
it's not an actual table, like you see here,

08:35.580 --> 08:38.970
but inside of it, it stores a bunch of entries,

08:38.970 --> 08:42.000
references, to blobs, and to other trees.

08:42.000 --> 08:43.530
Each one has a name,

08:43.530 --> 08:45.330
and each one has a hash,

08:45.330 --> 08:47.340
a blob hash, or a tree hash.

08:47.340 --> 08:49.650
And each tree, as we've seen, has a hash,

08:49.650 --> 08:51.930
and that's all I'll say for now about it.

08:51.930 --> 08:54.390
I know it's getting kind of complicated,

08:54.390 --> 08:56.943
but this is hopefully all gonna come together soon.
